## 13. Security Risks

### Do not confuse authentication with authorization

A successful sign-in does not mean the user has access to every Azure resource.

### Do not over-permission

Do not grant broad access simply to make the error disappear.

### Do not ignore inheritance

Higher-scope assignments may already provide access.

### Do not ignore groups

A group can provide Azure RBAC access even when the user has no direct assignment.

### Do not modify security controls unnecessarily

Troubleshoot the authorization path before changing tenant-wide security settings.

### Do not publish sensitive diagnostics

Sanitize IP addresses, session identifiers, request IDs, correlation IDs and other unnecessary diagnostic information before public publication.

---

## 14. Troubleshooting Decision Tree

```text
Did authentication succeed?
        |
   +----+----+
   |         |
  No        Yes
   |         |
Fix auth     v
         Is target resource correct?
                |
           +----+----+
           |         |
          No        Yes
           |         |
      Correct      v
              Is role assigned?
                    |
               +----+----+
               |         |
              No        Yes
               |         |
          Determine     v
          least role   Is scope correct?
                            |
                       +----+----+
                       |         |
                      No        Yes
                       |         |
                 Correct scope   v
                           Check inheritance,
                           group membership,
                           role actions and
                           Activity Log
```

---

## 15. Lessons Learned

**What failed?**

- 

**Did authentication succeed?**

- 

**What caused the authorization problem?**

- 

**What role and scope fixed it?**

- 

**Could the remediation be made narrower?**

- 

**What would I monitor in production?**

- 

